  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 42a7ef911f4c60e408989a759d2b9b18, type: 3}
  m_Name: PHP_IF_ELSE
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::CodeEscape.Programming.LessonData
  id: PHP_IF_ELSE
  language: 2
  conceptTitle: 'Conditions: if and else'
  symbol: if ( )
  relatedQuestionIds:
  - PHP_05_IF_ELSE
  - HZ_PHP_03_IF_ELSE
  - PHP_TYPE_05_IF_ELSE
  topics:
  - If / Else
  definition: 'An if statement makes a decision: it runs some code only when a condition
    is true.'
  howItWorks: In PHP the condition goes inside round brackets ( ) and the code to
    run goes inside curly braces { }. An else { } block runs when the condition is
    false. Use == to compare two values; a single = stores a value instead.
  exampleCode: "<?php\n$temperature = 30;\nif ($temperature > 25) {\n    echo \"Wear
    a hat\";\n} else {\n    echo \"Bring a jacket\";\n}\n?>"
  exampleExplanation: 30 > 25 is true, so the code inside the first { } runs and
    outputs Wear a hat. The else block is skipped. If $temperature were 18, the output
    would be Bring a jacket.
  tip: 'PHP comparison operators: == (equal), != (not equal), <, >, <= and >=.'
